WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.000
This is the RunShore console on the local Kubernetes scope: platform status on the left, activity in the centre, database and observability controls on the right.

2
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
The Containers tab lists every pod in the cluster. All 13 are healthy.

3
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Switching scope asks first. Switching only changes the view: nothing running is stopped or destroyed.

4
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:44.000
AWS, Azure and Google Cloud are scopes too. No cloud cluster is deployed in this run, so they have nothing to show.

5
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:55.000
Back on local Kubernetes, the cluster is unchanged.

6
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Inspect opens a pod's full description: its status, where it runs, its containers and their settings.

7
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:47.000
The Scoreboard shows every database on every scope: the latest result of each test, pass rates and how long each test takes.

8
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Resources shows the CPU and memory each container is using.

9
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
Ask AI answers questions about the platform from its live state and diagnostics.

10
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Learn holds lessons on the technology behind RunShore, and a library of 132 fixes, incidents and guides recorded while building it. Ask AI draws on the same material.

11
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:41.000
The Operations Console opens the Kubernetes Monitor: pods, nodes, resource usage, events and pod logs.

12
00:02:41.000 --> 00:03:29.000
The Apps tab runs real client applications on the platform's databases. Only PostgreSQL is deployed in this run, so the apps for MySQL, MongoDB and SAP ASE show as not deployed.

